    abstract = {Interaction in English as a lingua franca (ELF) has become a commonplace phenomenon. This article revisits research on ELF to discuss what it means for speakers to operate in different types of ELF interactions and how ELF use is impacting on the forms of English. Findings suggest, for instance, the importance of accommodation and enhanced explicitness in spoken ELF interaction as means to increase the effectiveness of communication. Despite the variability of ELF, there is also evidence for renewed preference patterns in ELF use, and even some indication of ELF impact on functional varieties of English.},
