@incollection{ISHFAQ2025223,
    abstract = {Green molecular design (GMD) is an emerging field focused on creating chemical processes and products that are environmentally sustainable and minimize toxicological risks. This chapter provides an extensive overview of the computational tools and approaches used in GMD to reduce toxicological risks in chemical compounds. The importance of this field lies in its potential to transform chemical manufacturing by reducing the environmental impact, minimizing hazardous substance use, and promoting resource efficiency. Several computational tools, including machine learning (ML), first-principles simulation, and genetic algorithms (GAs), are discussed here, highlighting their key features and capabilities. The importance of combining computational predictions with experimental data to ensure the practical applicability of designed molecules is also stressed. Besides, case studies and applications demonstrate the practical utility of these tools in developing eco-friendly methodologies and products. The chapter highlights the principles of green chemistry and the strategies for molecular design that guide the development of safer chemicals. By examining current challenges and advancements, we provide insights into future directions for research, including the need for improved predictive toxicology tools and the inclusion of green chemistry principles into educational curricula. In summary, this chapter aims to inform and inspire further research and development in GMD, ultimately contributing to a more sustainable and safer chemical industry.},
