@incollection{SINGH2025253,
    abstract = {The field of contemporary drug discovery has changed as a result of the combination of structural biology and computer-aided drug design (CADD). High-resolution insights into the structure and dynamics of biological macromolecules have been made possible by structural biology techniques like nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectroscopy, small-angle X-ray scattering (SAXS), cryo-electron microscopy (cryo-EM), and X-ray crystallography. The exact identification of druggable sites, clarification of molecular interactions, and logical design of drugs and medicines have all been made possible by these structural discoveries. In addition to these experimental methods, CADD uses algorithms and computational tools to speed up drug discovery workflows. Researchers can anticipate ligand-binding affinities, optimize lead compounds, and evaluate their pharmacokinetic and pharmacodynamic profiles through molecular docking, pharmacophore modeling, virtual screening, molecular dynamics simulations, and free energy calculations. Furthermore, CADD's predictive powers have been enhanced by developments in artificial intelligence and machine learning, which have simplified the process of creating new medications with improved efficacy and fewer side effects. In this chapter, the applications of these fundamental computational techniques to support lead optimization, lead discovery, and target identification are discussed.},
