@incollection{NEMETH2025,
    abstract = {Natural language processing (NLP) opens new perspectives for sociology by automatically processing large amounts of textual data. Through a series of case studies, the chapter shows the potential of two main types of NLP techniques, supervised and unsupervised machine learning, with a focus on word embedding in the latter. In the context of supervised learning, the challenges of coding, the sociological interpretability of prediction performance, the importance of model interpretation, and the problem of causal inference are discussed. Through examples, it is demonstrated that automated solutions cannot replace the researcher, highlighting the relevance of domain knowledge and, more generally, the importance of aligning methodological paradigms at the intersection of data science and social science.},
